  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 308 AM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to lock onto a upper-level low forming \nover the Central Plains early Sunday associated with a negatively \ntilted trough overhead and southeast winds, both aloft and at the \nsurface. Medium confidence (50-60%) exists of a surface low pressure \nsystem south of the CWA (County Warning Area) on Sunday, but no \nfront is expected. Meanwhile, a ridge starts to build in over the \nMountain West which is likely to propagate across our area Monday \nthrough Wednesday. Guidance shows winds aloft from the northwest \nwhich become southwesterly as said ridge moves through. Model output \nstarts to become a little more fuzzy late Wednesday but most \nensembles are picking up another negatively tilted trough with a \nstrong upper-level jet streak. This would theoretically bring \nstronger surface winds as early as Tuesday, lasting through \nWednesday night. Guidance projects moisture advection to take place \nearly Sunday with dew points forecast in the low to mid 40s.\n\nRain will be possible on Sunday morning as the low moves to the \nsouth of our CWA. PoPs are currently sitting around 20-40% through \nthe afternoon hours. QPF is currently forecast somewhere in the \nrange of 0.01 to 0.1 so at the very least some accumulating rainfall \nis probable. Chances for rain decrease substantially heading into \nMonday as that ridge takes control. 5% or less PoPs are \nexpected through Tuesday. Rainfall chances present Wednesday as \nwell with the aforementioned second trough. Confidence on any \nrain occurring a week from now is on the lower side at the \nmoment with PoPs in the 15-25% range. Nevertheless, will need to\nbe monitored going into the weekend as ensembles sort out any \nuncertainties with this trough. Moving onto temperatures for our\nregion, a warming trend is expected heading into next week as \nthe ridge builds upon the Central CONUS. Highs in the mid to \nupper 40s should increase to the upper 50s/low 60s by Wednesday.\nMorning lows are anticipated to sit around freezing on Monday \nand Tuesday then increase a bit into the 35-42 range on \nWednesday.\n\nFire weather should not be a concern through early next week. \nDespite a recent dry spell, coupled with stronger stronger surface \nwinds forecasted for the Monday-Wednesday timeframe, Relative \nHumidities (RHs) are not likely to be low. It should be on the \nwetter side with a system pulling in moisture from the \nsoutheast. RHs in the range of 50-80% during peak heating will \nbe optimal for keeping fire weather at bay. Wednesday will \nlikely be the driest day as temperatures warm and dewpoints \nremain stagnant but RHs shouldn't dip below 40%. As mentioned \nabove, surface winds pick up through the middle of next week as \na sustained 15-25 mph is forecasted. Gusts may reach 40 mph in \nsome areas of eastern Colorado.",
